#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e12db33-cf16-5171-9f31-62a450f113d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e12db33-cf16-5171-9f31-62a450f113d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256522} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca6a3251-a073-53a9-83e3-b4e5a74fd2c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36d75191-edf3-543e-8a14-a081f1ebcbf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-101287" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}-4}{2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{5x+6}-4}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6}-4)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(\\sqrt{5x+6})^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x+6-16}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5x-10}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5(x-2)}{(x-2)(\\sqrt{5x+6}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5x+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5(2)+6}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74ff2f8-7620-5e16-8c0c-5130f766deff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c74ff2f8-7620-5e16-8c0c-5130f766deff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,1,3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256631} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca9eb716-5caa-52e1-80e8-963f5aee39ca" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256630}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ead3adb-42a6-500f-89f0-27da9f251d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-104021" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25dbe28eeed5a93d9813f6eab6fd73ebade61bfb2b7a76c933b61cdde46e78e6.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=-2,1$ and $3,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,1,3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff9486fe-3ee1-5917-88de-97251c889552" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function is Left- or Right-Continuous From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b715ad1a-ee55-5582-a671-bbfdbb363d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b715ad1a-ee55-5582-a671-bbfdbb363d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256643} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfed29c-0003-53c7-9de5-749accda86d0" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256642}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84f94ec2-29d7-53da-9169-3c19a469e54b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104282" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/6a250c587a1c12845759eefdda74faac240c7cec2c63e1d6a6180b1b09292637.png)\n\nAt which points is the function $y=f(x)$ above not continuous but is right-continuous?\n\n$x$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the right-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nThe function has three discontinuities: $x=-2$, $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- At $x=-2$  the function is neither left- nor right-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function is not right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=0\\ne -1=f(0).\n$$\n\n- At $x=2$ the function is right-continuous because\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=-2=f(2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ff054f-4330-5216-be2a-d8a2342715bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50ff054f-4330-5216-be2a-d8a2342715bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256677} :answer-field/id #uuid "128d9980-d249-5545-9983-717c12b6222b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b1b8ae9-0781-5016-a848-e131e2cd0081" :question/math-academy-id "q-106723" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{3}≫\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}(x+2)}-20)=\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5392be73-c2fc-5483-9a69-e3f002bb5aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5392be73-c2fc-5483-9a69-e3f002bb5aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256680} :answer-field/id #uuid "294a7179-3399-56aa-8698-72d45ca63d34" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cab1e7b8-4832-542d-b640-2493abb21591" :question/math-academy-id "q-107059" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5-1}-2}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}-2}{x-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x-1}-2)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x-1})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-1-4}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{x-5}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{x-1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5-1}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "619182bf-107d-5217-a306-ec4f7c1109eb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Function Given in Implicit Form Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e608401-e3fb-53b0-b54b-05e7ec4e2463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e608401-e3fb-53b0-b54b-05e7ec4e2463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256768} :answer-field/id #uuid "481e1c47-35b5-5102-af61-c60cf880797a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3ec1c97-2b02-5dff-90e9-709a0139d59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108660" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for ${x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}=8x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3} & =8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y-4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ [{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy]-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}) & =8-2xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{8-2xy}{{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d7e6958-14c6-585b-a067-97090886ba60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7e6958-14c6-585b-a067-97090886ba60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256794} :answer-field/id #uuid "328a0aad-9468-5453-9137-15073cf0ac80" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7308db96-948c-5f8f-99e0-2a51c699fda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-110047" :question/problem "If $h(x)={3}^{2x+4},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={3}^{2x+4},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={3}^{2y+4}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({3}^{2y+4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={3}^{2y+4}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}3\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={3}^{2(y+2)}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}({3}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={9}^{y+2}\\operatorname{ln}9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{9}^{y+2}\\operatorname{ln}9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{9}^{-y}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{9}^{-{h}^{-1}(x)}}{81\\operatorname{ln}9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211dd85d-2832-52ce-8e1e-34f5dc81217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "211dd85d-2832-52ce-8e1e-34f5dc81217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814} :answer-field/id #uuid "652c9ef4-abcb-555e-bd23-6001869c8931" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256813}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03236a87-fcdc-5f26-80c5-0425c864d161" :question/math-academy-id "q-110234" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(4).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| $1$ | $4$ | $-2$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=1$ and ${f}^{'}(1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9e3c5ab-0da9-504a-888f-7bfd23b7937a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e3c5ab-0da9-504a-888f-7bfd23b7937a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256820} :answer-field/id #uuid "c07f2208-d0a3-5e70-9c26-0d4c4b241668" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1dda7663-69d0-5c1d-b3aa-82594e0c8df7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110328" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3x+\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2-3x \\\\ x & =4-12x+9{x}^{2} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-13x+4 & =0 \\\\ (9x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{9},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{4}{9}$ satisfies the equation $3x+\\sqrt{x}=2.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(2)=\\frac{4}{9}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{9})}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =3+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9}) & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(\\frac{4}{9})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709b6546-0492-5232-93ce-5810ccae4867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709b6546-0492-5232-93ce-5810ccae4867" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871} :answer-field/id #uuid "f64d4b93-4068-5741-b0af-7a069f7f3785" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d895573-240c-53b0-84aa-628f79fd54ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-112579" :question/problem "The budget of a school varies over the year. The budget $B,$ in thousands of dollars, after $t$ months since the beginning of the school can be modeled by the function $B(t)=(t-1)^{2}{e}^{-t}+10$ thousands of dollars. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the budget as a function of $t?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the budget is equal to ${B}^{'}(t).$ \n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{'}(t) & =2(t-1){e}^{-t}-(t-1)^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(2t-2-{t}^{2}+2t-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the school budget is ${e}^{-t}(4t-3-{t}^{2})$ thousands of dollars per month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "078c9361-898f-5b9f-889e-c92442f882d9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Factor Rule to Integrate a Constant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e0a3c5-10ee-5686-be92-765de4fb495d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e0a3c5-10ee-5686-be92-765de4fb495d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256985} :answer-field/id #uuid "db136630-c656-5fea-a964-52c000eee69b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae7da13b-8fe6-5af8-ba08-e04d213a5d46" :question/math-academy-id "q-113742" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-5)\\,\\text{d}y & =-5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =-5\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =-5y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cab27fd-3069-5cc6-afec-9864eda48a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cab27fd-3069-5cc6-afec-9864eda48a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256988} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d5778e-c306-5142-96da-31b0909bd75a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73897e00-bd3f-5a2c-b0a5-3ce83bc59e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-113781" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[4]{x}-{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{(5+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{(6)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b85d035c-ce66-5d55-8159-c3de5b3fe4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b85d035c-ce66-5d55-8159-c3de5b3fe4ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257009} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a9c9fc-6d99-5e9c-a617-fc21b7aa4cce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ef22d3-07d6-525f-b1e0-653bfae49fd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113886" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9282bab5-deb0-5c96-8746-c1502da05608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9282bab5-deb0-5c96-8746-c1502da05608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257018} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25d2f28-6227-5300-8084-fcf842ad6d18" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1804c8ea-5802-59e5-92d9-206715562863" :question/math-academy-id "q-113927" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45048763-18a9-5264-ab1c-dabdb5d833c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45048763-18a9-5264-ab1c-dabdb5d833c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257085} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8edd50f-599e-5978-9f52-899e36acd0e2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35cad922-9f73-5ba1-b352-62472240f419" :question/math-academy-id "q-114253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{'}}{[8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{8\\sqrt{x+1}-2x-10} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{'}}{[2(x+1)^{-1/2}-1]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50fbfbd6-0fca-5aa3-bfda-d1b0e47fdc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50fbfbd6-0fca-5aa3-bfda-d1b0e47fdc04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257154} :answer-field/id #uuid "3954b683-3c10-54a5-a17c-d7be4469cc36" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66349321-873f-5a15-988a-584637cc50c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-11602" :question/problem "If $y=2{x}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=2{x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7})+(2{x}^{3})(\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}+\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=2} & =6(2)^{2}\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}+\\frac{4(2)^{4}}{\\sqrt{2(2)^{2}+7}} \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{15}+\\frac{64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{360+64}{\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{424}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d038d527-f95c-5714-972a-e967c6f9f5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d038d527-f95c-5714-972a-e967c6f9f5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257166} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae21e4d-4efb-5b65-9186-e6c8a1d3c95c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257165}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99dc60bd-f005-5d9a-815d-b21949c9faa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-116627" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=(x+1)^{3}$ between $x=0$ and $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-0}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(0+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{4}{n}{(\\frac{4j}{n}+1)}^{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4211aec-a9bd-5be3-80d7-a4777c57bb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4211aec-a9bd-5be3-80d7-a4777c57bb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257338} :answer-field/id #uuid "63317a9b-966c-5694-a976-c22ff086d1d3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257337}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dac1407f-85ee-5e7c-bd88-0868af1543b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-11939" :question/problem "Given that $y=x\\operatorname{ln}3x,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{ln}3x)+(x)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50f64b3-2e2a-557c-bffc-304beb447550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50f64b3-2e2a-557c-bffc-304beb447550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257839} :answer-field/id #uuid "994bc45e-12a8-51c1-a1ea-6029cb1bf4be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257838}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d19938b-2667-5667-8ce7-7a27acfa3c8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131287" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76450310-bc86-5f3b-9bb8-82206d89c627" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Left and Right Limits at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e334ffef-75e9-5487-9f3a-77355634a401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e334ffef-75e9-5487-9f3a-77355634a401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257842} :answer-field/id #uuid "27dad36a-52c3-50b4-a3c5-7ee3df9f5369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257841} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c598337d-157e-5bbe-8042-0760485016e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c598337d-157e-5bbe-8042-0760485016e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257844} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f502a90-720e-5765-9320-6a3936f57944" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257843}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ff2b93a-a0bc-53af-ab2c-ee40e773dd3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131292" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea4038bf-34f0-58cc-a7b6-6a4c1890570f" :knowledge-point/title "The Limit at Infinity for an Unbounded Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dffbcc-a431-5f43-ba88-17d3f5929f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08dffbcc-a431-5f43-ba88-17d3f5929f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257847} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea32915-546d-54ed-99cc-98c0d5b089df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "808f1015-6891-5a09-93ab-404c85659190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "808f1015-6891-5a09-93ab-404c85659190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257849} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d428796-dbd3-5a4b-93fb-06bdd0b671ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4561523f-428e-5eae-b015-b8c44c4fd383" :question/math-academy-id "q-131293" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $4$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=4.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute A Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c581f746-f4b4-565e-828e-9a9c760c5c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c581f746-f4b4-565e-828e-9a9c760c5c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257852} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9fd745-e806-5ad6-895e-42e00c4eb393" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "623629e1-214d-5c86-b00a-3914400f8f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-131295" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}) & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b94d5612-763f-59d8-9a80-7f6e2f18fa6c" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Constant Rule to Compute a Limit Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d315cf83-135e-579e-a342-0047cef82766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d315cf83-135e-579e-a342-0047cef82766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257855} :answer-field/id #uuid "81345881-d6c3-5516-a270-9dc397aaefa6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad6f060b-5cea-5986-996d-da86ba31d49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131296" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot 2 & =6 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345f47e2-55a9-53ee-b1e4-65537b163cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345f47e2-55a9-53ee-b1e4-65537b163cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257858} :answer-field/id #uuid "111b6e45-7881-53ea-9f81-b27ffe1c218f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257857}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40aecb4c-2e61-5d25-88a2-3f0a2a1cdd82" :question/math-academy-id "q-131299" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2b72a2-c486-5a7a-a65e-57e83b78f6cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Limit at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8468ab8a-35f5-5465-92dd-649276399934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8468ab8a-35f5-5465-92dd-649276399934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257861} :answer-field/id #uuid "8232e659-27a5-507f-bd15-b8a46dc6e6cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e174c22e-176b-564d-874b-bf6323795bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-131320" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b34b8521-9fd5-518c-8ea6-3f5b18e43f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34b8521-9fd5-518c-8ea6-3f5b18e43f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257864} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7438fd0-3b41-5961-8782-b5d7f5ecf71d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99802942-acec-5b70-82d3-4643460a87e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99802942-acec-5b70-82d3-4643460a87e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257866} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee8d153-f1d7-52a2-bfdf-7f602f427519" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257865}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b49288e9-8609-57aa-a4ab-46cd78301ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131334" :question/problem "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5496481-da3a-5232-86a1-a8d68543f8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5496481-da3a-5232-86a1-a8d68543f8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258341} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf936430-fd7e-59e3-b9d8-e58acbfdf8d1" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c9953ff-73b4-518b-b4dc-26aa0d298c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c9953ff-73b4-518b-b4dc-26aa0d298c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258343} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e8469ce-cf6a-5704-a82a-7c9d2074a871" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258342}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2cf6e87b-e52d-5fbd-84ff-b23aaed621ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-14697" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dc45107eb076c0dc199718def7bb3a34b73ad61ca00bda61e09472a9ea589c84.png)\n\nThe equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$  represents a circle. The tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ on the circle intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{x}{y})}_{(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},\\,\\sqrt{2})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-\\sqrt{2}) & =-1(x-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(x-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =-(0-\\sqrt{2})+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6ccb33-929d-5295-997a-ab2f62a93871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f6ccb33-929d-5295-997a-ab2f62a93871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258417} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ffa8c7-b03d-5de4-93f5-8a30fe004b83" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fbbb0cf-47c7-5324-a25f-d63e213ac5e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15640" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3(x-2)^{3}$ and the point $P(3,3)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(3).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3(x-2)^{3} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =9(x-2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{'}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,3)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(3,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dad9bdc2-a243-5437-8904-9906c2d9a90e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "968285cf-0e5f-56f0-b799-6231645ea515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "968285cf-0e5f-56f0-b799-6231645ea515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258588} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6539da-3322-59a4-a724-4cfc95dd35f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c3bccb0-4d19-5e29-b495-d2fd9d30979b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166175" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(-x-1) & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}x-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}1 \\\\  & =-(3)-1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6f3c71-d11b-59af-bad6-5f5c0ff6634f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a60774-5d9b-5718-807e-d54dbf557901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59a60774-5d9b-5718-807e-d54dbf557901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258591} :answer-field/id #uuid "edaee8b1-9c73-58df-9133-2852fecfb66c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258590}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71c08803-7449-5a1f-967b-740eb77c9f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166176" :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\,2\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,h\\,).$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-(x+h)^{2}-(2x-{x}^{2})}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-{x}^{2}-2xh-{h}^{2}-2x+{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h-2hx-{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(2-2x-h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dad9bdc2-a243-5437-8904-9906c2d9a90e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e22463b7-3f0f-5292-9a84-6f7573b5b2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22463b7-3f0f-5292-9a84-6f7573b5b2bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258594} :answer-field/id #uuid "201b11e5-1c48-52a4-9da0-0b9795eaec60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258593}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe503c51-2ef0-5949-be80-1be073db8d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-166177" :question/problem "${f}^{'}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x-h) & ={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x)-{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =2-2x-0 \\\\  & =2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b9b9b9d-39a0-5ab6-9093-f6af0e0fb006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b9b9b9d-39a0-5ab6-9093-f6af0e0fb006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258597} :answer-field/id #uuid "9705d313-ba96-5e54-999b-f76dc9705e0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258596}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a327301f-c4a9-5bc3-8650-35e0d214db46" :question/math-academy-id "q-166178" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/17c57ba55e06207350ab93c8cac79bf22c48ba94e73ce4f914cd809b74b7d862.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n$A\\,\\approx \\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\approx (\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx (0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14882465-44da-51bd-9828-96748b5d62c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14882465-44da-51bd-9828-96748b5d62c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258600} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4e4b5a3-a5dc-57ea-b36b-782656ca2ec3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258599}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea206d90-e898-5894-9e09-0438d3e9d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-166179" :question/problem "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-$\n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $n=4$ subintervals, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $Δx=0.5.$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 0.5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5f(0.5i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(2(0.5i)-{(0.5i)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-0.25{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c192d0e-7507-5afd-b661-aa7f8b5845b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c192d0e-7507-5afd-b661-aa7f8b5845b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258603} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f0467e-235b-5cfb-858b-72cd6471c495" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e57e6f80-98ec-51f0-b4ef-4ce8ebf21cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e57e6f80-98ec-51f0-b4ef-4ce8ebf21cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258605} :answer-field/id #uuid "760b48b0-8fc8-58d1-af9c-815eabc68371" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9ba5916-8581-5cae-8448-1a538a55cd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ba5916-8581-5cae-8448-1a538a55cd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258607} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd4e733-f0a6-58c4-aa97-f25abd24650f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258606}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a45ff5-8464-5ba2-820e-b08ce09f5a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166180" :question/problem "The exact value of the area $A$ from part 7 can be expressed using definite integral notation as\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We can express the area $A$ as the definite integral of \n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nbetween \n\n* the lower limit $x=0,$ and \n\n* the upper limit $x=2.$ \n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115f0f29-ce77-523c-9e5e-b41e648e8037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "115f0f29-ce77-523c-9e5e-b41e648e8037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258616} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d365fe-6cf5-561b-a054-3ad8b527bfc9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c0f667a-d499-5b4f-9cda-30ad08f61832" :question/math-academy-id "q-16698" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{cos}3x)+({e}^{x})(-3\\operatorname{sin}3x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}3x-3{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}3x \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}3x-3\\operatorname{sin}3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe05aa67-e29e-5803-980c-e49ecb5db248" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle at a Particular Moment Given Its Position"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4e18b1b-d9d7-56d0-aea6-f9fa6cfa1eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e18b1b-d9d7-56d0-aea6-f9fa6cfa1eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258803} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d0e3494-3f35-5a2b-a913-8b989f1cc752" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258802}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb087fc5-5c8e-5daa-8c21-50c3794c633b" :question/math-academy-id "q-19455" :question/problem "The displacement of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=2{e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{ln}t-4t.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{ln}t-4t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{t}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{t}-4) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a087fc-2640-5da9-891d-8d1a3aca9168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a087fc-2640-5da9-891d-8d1a3aca9168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258832} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9fbe8e7-03e3-57f1-8d03-b8f737b529ba" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258831}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a791af4-1cbd-592a-a199-1e3e85cf8cfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-20183" :question/problem "A ball is thrown up in the air. After $t$ seconds, its height is $h(t)=-16{t}^{2}+54t+10$ feet above the ground.\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is equal to ${h}^{'}(1.5).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-16{t}^{2}+54t+10) \\\\  & =-32t+54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1.5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(1.5) & =-32(1.5)+54 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the height when $t=1.5$ seconds is $6$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum and Constant Factor Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6394dc33-e30b-58fb-9e52-5d1c89ba1367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6394dc33-e30b-58fb-9e52-5d1c89ba1367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258927} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee27e183-57b1-58b5-b807-de1ab0dfe349" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d243d5f8-0920-5cfd-89d2-27ebf1aa8e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-2251" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $3x+6.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x+6\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+6(x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "007f3355-427a-5da4-8af7-22581f1cb1de" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sums of Power Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1297fb4-161d-57ae-b361-48fedfb15d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1297fb4-161d-57ae-b361-48fedfb15d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258930} :answer-field/id #uuid "12535024-6340-5b46-ba41-d4cfdf50fe70" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258929}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b330cce-24ca-5db6-b251-1e9ced5aba72" :question/math-academy-id "q-2252" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+(\\frac{x}{2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64c2707e-63f1-58c0-955d-2d56bc061b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c2707e-63f1-58c0-955d-2d56bc061b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef3ff14a-b639-579b-abc0-3ff06e28509d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58674690-b2aa-5ed0-bc5d-80ece4fc7632" :question/math-academy-id "q-2437" :question/problem "Research of a factory assembly line shows that the number of units $f$ produced in $x$ hours can be modeled by the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+20x.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of production when $x=2$ hours?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of production when $x=2$ hours is equal to ${f}^{'}(2).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+20x) \\\\  & =2x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(2) & =2(2)+20 \\\\  & =4+20 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of production when $x=2$ hours is $24$ units per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0977a4b8-c55e-516c-8efd-e325db979b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0977a4b8-c55e-516c-8efd-e325db979b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259053} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e8d870a-608a-5711-ab2f-7b05fd031af2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259052}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b93b20-63af-5abb-b512-c02cecaedf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-2545" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f59f65e184ccf4fdf2ae2124543ece45d41afce09d743dec9f17730dd00124d4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circle above is increasing. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r.$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a58c0a18-4221-50f1-b31c-ae58d099d79d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum and Constant Factor Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af25d7e7-ed8c-5fc7-989e-6efc6599d586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af25d7e7-ed8c-5fc7-989e-6efc6599d586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259347} :answer-field/id #uuid "67c13983-cf41-5067-8f92-042471b1b008" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2d5bd7-e7c7-50ca-8707-a9c29a9151da" :question/math-academy-id "q-29825" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{-2})+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{-2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40674303-c84e-53da-aa07-9a9b9e2be9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40674303-c84e-53da-aa07-9a9b9e2be9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259362} :answer-field/id #uuid "c85b5b07-734a-5431-a36d-96c50ba5499f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259361}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "384b4b4a-1a21-5de5-b09c-7d8dcb9b51ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-300969" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{\\,3{x}^{4}+5\\,}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8)}{{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}(2x+8) & =2{lim}_{x\\to -1}x+{lim}_{x\\to -1}8 \\\\  & =2(-1)+8 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -1}(3{x}^{4}+5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3(-1)^{4}+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{3{x}^{4}+5}} & =\\frac{6}{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70779499-2c86-54f2-81ee-5504191f9ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70779499-2c86-54f2-81ee-5504191f9ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259374} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba39c0e5-abab-533a-9258-7d7166fa507e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259373}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83f4f818-d835-5987-9636-e45c64049c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-302415" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bd45ff-27ae-5ca3-a764-8f5b76e15666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31bd45ff-27ae-5ca3-a764-8f5b76e15666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259377} :answer-field/id #uuid "43f94853-1c0e-5c4c-9db2-3cf5d1d70da9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259376}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001d04f8-b07b-5927-bb3f-243c2de00cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-302430" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}}4\\operatorname{cot}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0efb74e-6dc4-52d8-b4f1-9c5676d9a231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0efb74e-6dc4-52d8-b4f1-9c5676d9a231" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259420} :answer-field/id #uuid "23224884-d1e7-55e4-b946-c8bc01c96771" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259419}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9a78083-f539-5f87-8b3e-c72bf4128486" :question/math-academy-id "q-31241" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}+2x-1)$ at $x=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is given by ${f}^{'}(0).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}u,\\,u={x}^{2}+2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}u\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}u \\\\  & =(2x+2)\\operatorname{cos}({x}^{2}+2x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(0) & =(2\\cdot 0+2)\\operatorname{cos}({0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=0$ is $2\\operatorname{cos}1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ff98e6-667e-58bd-98fc-d22141a55c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ff98e6-667e-58bd-98fc-d22141a55c7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44d9737-467b-5c51-8ca8-3576f2404f16" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259428}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f6b45e4-aad3-52ce-a5c4-dc96f14dd157" :question/math-academy-id "q-31272" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{'}}{[{x}^{2}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{'}}{[2x]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20b04ed-9f25-54bb-9a7c-af2f68180637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac\\pi2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d20b04ed-9f25-54bb-9a7c-af2f68180637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac\\pi2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259438} :answer-field/id #uuid "592c960a-7917-538b-9b05-b6908b3d02b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d32428de-c244-5f32-a35a-7db55d2d68e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-313299" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=2x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{sin}2x)+({x}^{2})(2\\operatorname{cos}2x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}2x+2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74c4b0d3-8dd1-599f-9f63-02f09520a7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+2i-{i}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74c4b0d3-8dd1-599f-9f63-02f09520a7cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8+2i-{i}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259468} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8e4c9b6-7c52-5c61-a079-5e5b766d8862" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259467}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2c521c2-d305-533c-b589-d2a02598a150" :question/math-academy-id "q-315704" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ca91f5bd9354efe22a5782fb7aee7c4a147b5ae186a661fbc4f77aa982af28c8.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=9-{x}^{2}$ between $x=-1$, $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n${S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(-1+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[9-(i-1)^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[9-({i}^{2}-2i+1)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(8+2i-{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903adbd9-a10f-58fb-8a98-e6e37b7ed681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903adbd9-a10f-58fb-8a98-e6e37b7ed681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f256b11-f212-5064-a478-a91d70be3eda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e75c3d7-59f2-5707-90f3-3f9ef53a3d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e75c3d7-59f2-5707-90f3-3f9ef53a3d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259497} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0a3a4c-f1aa-5bd4-b510-60c3ff25d562" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34d0f7c4-2abe-5df5-963b-378be7040284" :question/math-academy-id "q-315828" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={e}^{\\,{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8f67de-5aae-59a6-ade5-f44783342ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}(4{f}^{-1}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd8f67de-5aae-59a6-ade5-f44783342ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}(4{f}^{-1}(x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd27c77e-9e5d-5327-80f6-9f1ae988bd0c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259618}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "506e531d-5777-544e-ae01-6ba51aecd58b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32634" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(4x)+5,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cos}(4x)+5,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}(4y)+5\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cos}(4y)+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}(4y)\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}(4y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}(4y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}(4y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}(4{f}^{-1}(x)) \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}(4{f}^{-1}(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f64d46-9f19-54a3-ac8e-a7cd4956bbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5{h}^{-1}(x)-1}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f64d46-9f19-54a3-ac8e-a7cd4956bbc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5{h}^{-1}(x)-1}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622} :answer-field/id #uuid "87509928-4c83-533c-88a1-c2eea0388b62" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259621}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4b70c3-5b64-5323-b3fa-be629ae754ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-32640" :question/problem "If $h(x)=\\sqrt{5x-1},$ find ${({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${h}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\sqrt{5x-1},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5y-1}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={h}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{5y-1}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}((5y-1)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(5y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5y-1}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5y-1}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${h}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={h}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{h}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5{h}^{-1}(x)-1}}{5} \\\\ {({h}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5{h}^{-1}(x)-1}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8ca8cc8-40fb-5b61-956e-ab3c8ddf2f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}(2{f}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ca8cc8-40fb-5b61-956e-ab3c8ddf2f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}(2{f}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259692} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ce2b902-e5bf-5ba7-a987-8f030fd802fa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e60f9d54-34c5-598e-aa94-89662e26fd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-3299" :question/problem "If $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}(2x-1),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=3\\operatorname{tan}(2x-1),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}(2y-1)\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3\\operatorname{tan}(2y-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =3{sec}^{2}(2y-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6{sec}^{2}(2y-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6{sec}^{2}(2y-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}(2y-1)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}(2{f}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}(2{f}^{-1}(x)-1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbfe9c99-a15e-56d3-a022-b2e5b5db6c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbfe9c99-a15e-56d3-a022-b2e5b5db6c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810} :answer-field/id #uuid "247ff0b5-77b8-534b-9382-37f314bfbdc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ccc6a25-2db7-5706-96d9-17776fd59433" :question/math-academy-id "q-338440" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =1+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098095c4-58e8-5d0b-b380-d18f864b68d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "098095c4-58e8-5d0b-b380-d18f864b68d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813} :answer-field/id #uuid "38935977-02b1-5ee5-979d-5436f0462ccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259812}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dc64186-1928-5101-a754-746877780e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-338444" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ directly into the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}csc\\,(\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d73477-f34f-5580-b9b7-c8a24304e0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac3{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74d73477-f34f-5580-b9b7-c8a24304e0bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac3{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259819} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b70a58-b70f-5293-b8fb-3650a675967b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259818}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5098a209-5e2a-59a9-840f-1d112ceb224b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338485" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & =\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3(0)+25}}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3x+25})(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x+25})}^{2}}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{25-(3x+25)}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3x}{x(5+\\sqrt{3x+25})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3x+25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{5+\\sqrt{3(0)+25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3764b5-0eaf-586c-8cf3-8754e6fd6227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3764b5-0eaf-586c-8cf3-8754e6fd6227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259825} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b5640e3-e135-59a8-88e8-a44d496816e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259824}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c248ff4-4847-5ae9-892d-0c9714b8f260" :question/math-academy-id "q-338517" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x+1)-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\,(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2) \\\\  & =6(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec1bfb4d-04a7-5bf7-8a25-61ed5a9845d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac4{(4x-9)\\ln7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec1bfb4d-04a7-5bf7-8a25-61ed5a9845d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac4{(4x-9)\\ln7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259926} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41e7443-79c1-5d2b-930e-5341569b9979" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259925}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f4d284a-2614-5126-9711-edae5b31e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-341686" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{7}(4x-9),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{7}(u),\\,u=4x-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(7)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(7)}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4x-9)\\operatorname{ln}(7)}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(4x-9)\\operatorname{ln}(7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7c615ab-9bbb-5955-bb3b-d6288046c2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7c615ab-9bbb-5955-bb3b-d6288046c2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259961} :answer-field/id #uuid "d402cc6b-4e43-5189-9911-69bfdb98f274" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29080498-4b3f-5a6b-865c-7ce59c1f650a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29080498-4b3f-5a6b-865c-7ce59c1f650a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259963} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bc9d3db-661a-55d1-91d6-eea4ac888220" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259962}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f330ff48-40f6-5605-a79f-2ec52c835e8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342222" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt[4]{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt[4]{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{1}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-2\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-2x+2+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,2,0\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94ad2fb7-2504-5816-a769-6ed44eb62170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94ad2fb7-2504-5816-a769-6ed44eb62170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259991} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8c24970-d441-5e3e-9559-5215250961ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4357f51-4421-5509-a2b9-31e5b3d982f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342566" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/e4f1c8624a4b40f21fa4ea3c3ea9df329498a9ce5b8fddcdfabc939e4bd97325.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(1,2)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{'}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{'}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(1,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65208e34-961f-5800-aecf-7913b9cc17dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-\\frac{1}{t+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65208e34-961f-5800-aecf-7913b9cc17dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-\\frac{1}{t+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260000} :answer-field/id #uuid "edd42039-f8ae-5c04-b077-f451182c6b56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d0f236f-7217-5ff9-8564-e8fe30009b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-343163" :question/problem "The number of plant species $S$ in a nature reserve at $t$ tens of kilometers from the coast is modeled by the function $S(t)=250+3{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(t+2)$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the number of plant species as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the number of plant species is\n{{field-1}}\nper ten kilometers." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the number of plant species is equal to ${S}^{'}(t).$ \n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[250+3{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}(t+2)] \\\\  & =6t-\\frac{1}{t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the number of plant species is $6t-\\frac{1}{t+2}$ per ten kilometers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd95ecc5-6ac0-586e-9cc5-2b62dea29d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd95ecc5-6ac0-586e-9cc5-2b62dea29d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260009} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec00fdc-db0e-52e5-b16e-207c792cbd50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d3d568-0ecc-580b-9cae-c3e4d67350b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343387" :question/problem "The function $E(v)=\\frac{6v+18}{v+2}$ models the price of electricity, in dollars, based on the number of customers $v$ (in thousands) served the previous month. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price of electricity when $v=4$ thousand customers?\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $v=4$ is\n{{field-1}} dollars per thousand customers." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $v=4$ thousand customers is equal to ${E}^{'}(4).$\n\nNow, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{'}(v) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(\\frac{6v+18}{v+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(6v+18)\\cdot (v+2)-(6v+18)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}v}(v+2)}{(v+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)\\cdot (v+2)-(6v+18)\\cdot (1)}{(v+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6v+12-6v-18}{(v+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{(v+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $v=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{'}(4) & =-\\frac{6}{((4)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{36} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $v=4$ thousand customers is $-\\frac{1}{6}$ dollars per thousand customers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f61a3a14-0c07-51eb-9344-ac13192d34fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f61a3a14-0c07-51eb-9344-ac13192d34fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260012} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8971eb6-0392-5d7b-9e72-0eb042f52673" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260011}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5645258a-90ef-5fd3-9082-3764e92661b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343394" :question/problem "The function $F(c)=\\frac{{c}^{2}+4c+12}{2c+1}$ for $0\\le c\\le 20$ models the parking fee, in dollars, based on the number of cars $c$ that used the garage the previous hour. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the parking fee when $c=2$ cars?\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $c=2$ cars is\n{{field-1}} dollars per car." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $c=2$ cars is equal to ${F}^{'}(2).$\n\nNow, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(c) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}c}(\\frac{{c}^{2}+4c+12}{2c+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}c}({c}^{2}+4c+12)\\cdot (2c+1)-({c}^{2}+4c+12)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}c}(2c+1)}{(2c+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2c+4)\\cdot (2c+1)-({c}^{2}+4c+12)\\cdot (2)}{(2c+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{c}^{2}+10c+4-2{c}^{2}-8c-24}{(2c+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{c}^{2}+2c-20}{(2c+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $c=2$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(2) & =\\frac{2(2)^{2}+2(2)-20}{(2(2)+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $c=2$ cars is $-\\frac{8}{25}$ dollars per car."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217b3cdf-d9a6-5d92-af20-1a2fc17eaac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "217b3cdf-d9a6-5d92-af20-1a2fc17eaac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260015} :answer-field/id #uuid "9addb752-1c9d-5aa6-8cbb-442afe3f39ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0a48171a-86f8-5d26-b8a7-cb6fe266dbbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343499" :question/problem "The surface area $A$ of a cube is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the cube is changing with respect to its side length at the moment when the side length of the cube is equal to $3\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its side length is $3$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the surface area of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(s)=6{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(6{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =6(2s) \\\\  & =12s,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=3$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{'}(3)=12(3)=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the cube is changing when its side length is $3\\,\\text{in}$, is $36$ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42fc074a-7383-52bf-ba1e-28e089fc3328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42fc074a-7383-52bf-ba1e-28e089fc3328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260027} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9c9b82-d932-5722-81bf-2ba9adfc95ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260026}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2415bb3d-1a53-5c64-8f02-077d85065f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344184" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x)) & =\\operatorname{sin}(0)+4\\operatorname{cot}(0) \\\\  & =0+4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}(0)}{\\operatorname{sin}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ec2bf84d52026890b6796c30f3cd772b7cb34230200acf40b6e19bce7d891343.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}x$ are negative and decrease without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}x=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $4$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n \n\n$$\n4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{cot}x=4\\cdot (-\\infty )=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf81072e-2865-5688-a7e4-0d9d9a446739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf81072e-2865-5688-a7e4-0d9d9a446739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260060} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa21a04d-5603-586a-af0e-a56c3d9e534c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcf8ba9a-336f-56fa-995e-b7e274eebb71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcf8ba9a-336f-56fa-995e-b7e274eebb71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260062} :answer-field/id #uuid "00a9f18a-9e11-582b-b8b2-8374b50ad9dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39856b13-0a5e-55ed-acd0-029752eae9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39856b13-0a5e-55ed-acd0-029752eae9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260064} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7e7ff6-51f4-5c8d-91d3-a957b2ecfc30" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33332675-fdeb-54bf-95a8-8c105352c310" :question/math-academy-id "q-346162" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/041c7a781ae975cd097aa8a38e28c151f3af10f1390e2b4c2add93429bac23de.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ between the lower limit $x=-2$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fce754a-e462-51d7-8c66-642357490a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fce754a-e462-51d7-8c66-642357490a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260067} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d62c554-4966-5634-be81-524a964dfbb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01d815d9-2fd3-531e-be96-0070e157fedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-346174" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{1/2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{n}=\\frac{9}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{9}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{9i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(\\,1+\\frac{9i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,1/2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b0e10b-3084-5663-a755-5d3bc454be9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03b0e10b-3084-5663-a755-5d3bc454be9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260070} :answer-field/id #uuid "404e461f-cf60-5516-a07c-5cc49b263f07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260069}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afa7e8bd-d1e6-51b9-8473-2329d1a319b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-346206" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[7($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=7{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[7{(\\,1+\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c02b898-1aa1-52df-aa4d-6305b77d4cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c02b898-1aa1-52df-aa4d-6305b77d4cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260414} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f948605-0438-5761-a387-1a77da15a514" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260413}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1b61dac-dcb8-5733-8ee2-72d827e766d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-4469" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}-3}{5-5}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x-1}-3}{x-5} & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{2x-1}-3)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-1-9}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2x-10}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2(x-5)}{(x-5)(\\sqrt{2x-1}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2x-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2(5)-1}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4b2d75-2e5e-5759-a73d-ce1d5a2549ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4b2d75-2e5e-5759-a73d-ce1d5a2549ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260443} :answer-field/id #uuid "d277c78c-1469-51f9-96ba-677522683231" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "678f5c58-bbd6-5e36-9285-ca3cd7002851" :question/math-academy-id "q-46833" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & =\\frac{2-2}{2(2)-\\sqrt{8(2)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{8x})(2x+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{8x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4{x}^{2}-8x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4x(x-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{8x}}{4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+\\sqrt{8(2)}}{4(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b68e11b3-a57c-5715-bb2f-6f584359c034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b68e11b3-a57c-5715-bb2f-6f584359c034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260446} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0581553-e79c-501c-a973-086be2342b5f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6fab19a-aae0-556f-9992-7a68d4d6de92" :question/math-academy-id "q-46839" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=8$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & =\\frac{8-8}{4-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{(4-\\sqrt{2x})(4+\\sqrt{2x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{{4}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{16-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{-2(x-8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2x}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2(8)}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7c80b8c-99b2-516c-92f6-6996a2326efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7c80b8c-99b2-516c-92f6-6996a2326efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260460} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c25169-4101-508e-a401-e9f2318a0312" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7310ec48-8cde-5be8-a175-14a7f9afbd15" :question/math-academy-id "q-47042" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x) & =\\operatorname{csc}(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/c858e8756e180eeefc965a760b9d59b0e369738dfd16b32b17b448ca0daa513e.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x)$ are positive and grow without bound. So,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSimilarly, as $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(3x)$ are negative and decrease without bound. So,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /3)^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}(3x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ff4626-a304-5c5d-a590-6920e36f3c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ff4626-a304-5c5d-a590-6920e36f3c7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260463} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb14d33-2172-566a-8006-02841f6205b2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c10226c-ef84-5cda-8872-1e74e05928af" :question/math-academy-id "q-47125" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{csc}0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/fcdebef9e1184a2850c91e9ff1c4b6f0b47ce12b8bad07772171a7304f79150a.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}x$ are negative and decrease without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}x=-\\infty \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e37b91f0-ea0a-58b0-98d0-8f3626578dd6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "768e6c4d-51ac-50c3-8a3b-f304acb6b550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "768e6c4d-51ac-50c3-8a3b-f304acb6b550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260466} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd9fade0-f663-5328-a0df-46c2650d18ad" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260465}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5d16e5f-a36f-55fb-8cca-84ea5e751606" :question/math-academy-id "q-47134" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}8x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $t=8x.$ In the limit $x\\to 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & \\to 8(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}8x={lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cot}t=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}t$ is given below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/023929a3b0d4d40c7bd86a22ea04965d63e455ea3fc8d524c84314cb8fefdaac.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1d0ecf3-5468-5f38-9a7e-05e3641260a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(3{f}^{-1}(x))}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d0ecf3-5468-5f38-9a7e-05e3641260a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(3{f}^{-1}(x))}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260522} :answer-field/id #uuid "be866a01-6949-54da-bbbb-634e3f6bec6d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ace057fc-f27d-5e1a-9af6-15a230a30edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-47989" :question/problem "If $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}(3x)+3,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=-\\operatorname{sin}(3x)+3,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{sin}(3y)+3\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\operatorname{sin}(3y)+3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-\\operatorname{cos}(3y)\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}(3y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}(3y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(3y)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(3{f}^{-1}(x))}{3} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(3{f}^{-1}(x))}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9c5c917-cd9a-5116-977a-f13e8461778d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260531} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ad4e6-a361-5f9f-afac-1dbed637a8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260532} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0521a6-cb41-51e5-98d9-bd0a4835723b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260533} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb5de3c-2a0f-5ffc-bb2d-47e383c8020a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260534} {:answer/id #uuid "00c33181-3288-5ae5-8ba3-6a6e11b11119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260535} {:answer/id #uuid "b595ea90-16a9-5607-b40c-a6b2148a8777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260536} {:answer/id #uuid "58bfed97-d29c-5d97-afdb-29266e0fe8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260537} {:answer/id #uuid "469095ff-9ad6-5993-92ea-1f7061e39341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b595ea90-16a9-5607-b40c-a6b2148a8777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac779f9e-c0a9-531b-b21a-ca5587c63114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56d623d9-78f0-5f10-8521-99d9ec2fc8e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-48063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/f5ba5f7cc2ea5aaf405db1abff7e6f28ed4378ba6a5ec88d73c6861ede1d75de.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. - $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\nII. - ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ exists\nIII. - $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ breaks at $x=2$,  it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "433ac237-32ff-591c-ab0d-e271f5beda30" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Polynomial Function and a Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99cf18cd-d6f1-5c96-9586-f2af15c7606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99cf18cd-d6f1-5c96-9586-f2af15c7606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260544} :answer-field/id #uuid "491ab714-6bce-5018-a106-c09c06b2cb82" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260543}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be4b5477-1b23-5f88-be13-f138d6d0f412" :question/math-academy-id "q-48216" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $3x≫\\operatorname{ln}x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{3x}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa3b461-2365-5c6a-8b5f-1a14cad7f232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi\\,\\mathrm{in}^2/\\mathrm{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa3b461-2365-5c6a-8b5f-1a14cad7f232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi\\,\\mathrm{in}^2/\\mathrm{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260582} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e6d3dc-0869-5c1c-bc5b-fc35e25fc93c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e9f5e03-89e1-560d-8854-0c3b942d5434" :question/math-academy-id "q-48723" :question/problem "The surface area $A$ of a sphere is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing with respect to its radius at the moment when the radius of the sphere is equal to $4\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that the surface area of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(r)=4\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =8\\pi r,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{'}(4)=8\\pi (4)=32\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing when its radius is $4\\,\\text{in}$, is $32\\pi $ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "007f3355-427a-5da4-8af7-22581f1cb1de" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sums of Power Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c3922f1-2f0d-57dc-a324-9db300114e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{3}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c3922f1-2f0d-57dc-a324-9db300114e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{5}+\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{3}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea9442d-e5da-5cf6-9d19-fb38d9413713" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db18238a-fc51-5865-8ca3-aaa8e71e44d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49173" :question/problem "$\\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+10{x}^{2}+25{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+10\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})+10(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})+(x)+C \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}+\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{3}+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "777ada17-74df-558c-8eb7-5eba2e110bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "777ada17-74df-558c-8eb7-5eba2e110bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260602} :answer-field/id #uuid "87a4bf30-e9a1-5c7c-9c08-9eae7b2d0bcf" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260601}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae0c14e-2353-57d2-951a-f4c503f5887d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49189" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7194c7f4-bbb5-5b64-bfb9-02c7d2811cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(16-{i}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7194c7f4-bbb5-5b64-bfb9-02c7d2811cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(16-{i}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260610} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fa7bda8-718c-5e7d-9f90-fdafbec0d4ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb70f5d3-7528-53c6-aa91-6966c5074c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-49260" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/eb29a63cda5e01de48a224a2802542d45c6b69ee035edfd5ae675ed074d88bdb.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=16-{x}^{2}$ between $x=0$, $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}(16-{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d05ddaa7-36bb-5245-9790-05e0e5b9d1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\sqrt{\\frac{5j}{n}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d05ddaa7-36bb-5245-9790-05e0e5b9d1b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\sqrt{\\frac{5j}{n}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260613} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cf714d0-a133-56e5-a293-fca7ff99ee09" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260612}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cb8716f6-3c22-54a1-bc3f-98777b2194cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-49366" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ between $x=-1$ and $x=4$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=4,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-(-1)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-1+j\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-1+\\frac{5j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1+\\frac{5j}{n})+1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\sqrt{\\frac{5j}{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6b26c0-f41e-56e3-9bb3-7bd483e8aab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b6b26c0-f41e-56e3-9bb3-7bd483e8aab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260659} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba51ce0d-964f-5fa5-81c2-970951106ba8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d99c943d-c7b7-53d6-8c63-62b556330e7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-5031" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+1)+3x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(0).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{2}+1)+3x \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}+3 \\\\ {f}^{'}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d54bce4-66fe-5868-9b54-f3f29c4c70bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d54bce4-66fe-5868-9b54-f3f29c4c70bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260687} :answer-field/id #uuid "525e8ca0-013c-569e-ab32-1ace667393ec" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "824454d8-43d8-5ebf-a55a-85d20223cc1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51348" :question/problem "If $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+3,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{2}+3,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =8y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{8y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{8{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{8{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c12057c2-bb53-5e66-ac67-04a6e406e7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}(4{g}^{-1}(x)+\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c12057c2-bb53-5e66-ac67-04a6e406e7cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}(4{g}^{-1}(x)+\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260690} :answer-field/id #uuid "473cf69a-3e4f-5eca-90b6-0c3ea1e56d53" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260689}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f004e747-fa40-510b-9a94-e1103a008e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-51351" :question/problem "If $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}(4x+\\pi ),$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{'}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{cos}(4x+\\pi ),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}(4y+\\pi )\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{cos}(4y+\\pi )] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}(4y+\\pi )\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}(4y+\\pi ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}(4y+\\pi )} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}(4y+\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}(4{g}^{-1}(x)+\\pi )}{4} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}(4{g}^{-1}(x)+\\pi )}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfdb93d1-d550-5939-8a47-99bd1d863a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Intercept of a Tangent or Normal Line to a Curve Given in Implicit Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90209f67-483c-5e31-a0ad-bdd02e85f44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90209f67-483c-5e31-a0ad-bdd02e85f44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260746} :answer-field/id #uuid "48897116-5235-5acf-94fe-bcd383de1cee" :answer-field/key "coordinate-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852d25b5-37c8-5917-8e19-a7a6fff4d1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "852d25b5-37c8-5917-8e19-a7a6fff4d1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260748} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b89e891-b1fa-58ac-b17a-34e1f3ff8d2e" :answer-field/key "coordinate-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260747}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91ee7106-7624-554e-a734-a1b55bccb844" :question/math-academy-id "q-53754" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=9.$ The normal line at the point $(3,\\,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nCoordinate 1: {{coordinate-1}}\n\nCoordinate 2: {{coordinate-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,\\,3).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6x-4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-6x}{-4y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,\\,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3x}{2y})}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(3,\\,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(-3)+3 \\\\ y & =2+3 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad10ca6b-d130-559b-a201-15127659de13" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity of a Square Root Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c912fc0-3705-5a1a-88b6-d61d7865e577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c912fc0-3705-5a1a-88b6-d61d7865e577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261075} :answer-field/id #uuid "239240f1-3aeb-5c74-a4ab-d43f74a21b8f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f97ea71b-2d26-565c-a5fc-bee1699ced73" :question/math-academy-id "q-67659" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt{x+3}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+3}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b03d3bc42d076a63937ab41d939c6d39ceca8bdb879c9ed58d583ab8a694a979.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9fa2866-1cbe-5677-9b08-d62b6d1533d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9fa2866-1cbe-5677-9b08-d62b6d1533d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261318} :answer-field/id #uuid "56cd7ce6-6a32-5937-b6b1-83d51e53d834" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06997fad-09a6-5bf2-96c9-5af17ff052ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-71169" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\text{d}x+4\\int {x}^{0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+4(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1})+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1/2}+4x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0db9b1c3-1e31-53f6-9fc2-1c611e2f9398" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Integral Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "345426ba-1b4c-5b2a-a574-62be48ab1ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "345426ba-1b4c-5b2a-a574-62be48ab1ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261321} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f083c0c-3a24-5c45-8eb6-55af7fa99c63" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bea4210-f300-55db-95a6-25d0828ce335" :question/math-academy-id "q-71173" :question/problem "$\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1})+\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f7c0ae-c727-596d-b5e9-acd5ce0e0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16f7c0ae-c727-596d-b5e9-acd5ce0e0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261388} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafa4e81-2f31-5079-8a06-e3131206cf76" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261387}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4310b88-b219-51d1-b627-f9ad0abc215f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72340" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{csc}x]}^{'}}{[{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}]^{'}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}\\cancel{(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{csc}x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "478be923-982e-5a80-9b24-d543e248a700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(10-\\frac{1}{2}{k}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "478be923-982e-5a80-9b24-d543e248a700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(10-\\frac{1}{2}{k}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261440} :answer-field/id #uuid "91221181-7299-51b0-a0fd-b6febe9b09aa" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261439}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c4179d5-5df2-5fb9-a0e5-db1fb0a714c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-73095" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/488bd565ee63918d15d360328c0424e1321f327f883c043d266700cc48da1fcd.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=10-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}$ between $x=0$, $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(0+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(10-\\frac{1}{2}{k}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8405f090-3555-56ea-bf43-9d4c3a679bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}{(2+k)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8405f090-3555-56ea-bf43-9d4c3a679bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}{(2+k)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261469} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a682e81-d2c1-5d99-9bb5-ec2d5cb3164f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261468}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba0afa01-467d-51c1-a80b-556ade26c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73138" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/08a6d3eb52a4972cae07c1850a99d1b02a44a3f7d397836dee6b613de24f1f4c.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y={x}^{2}$ between $x=2$, $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=2$ and $b=5,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(2+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(2+k) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}{(2+k)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fee957b4-b31d-58b4-967f-94065dc1e1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}({(4+\\frac{5i}{n})}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fee957b4-b31d-58b4-967f-94065dc1e1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}({(4+\\frac{5i}{n})}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261472} :answer-field/id #uuid "b520c8dc-4870-5cb8-8859-eaf21e9bf9d2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3be2255-2f0e-5d27-8b7a-fbae4d52e2f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73158" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3},$ the lower bound is $a=4,$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(4+i\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(4+\\frac{5i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}({(4+\\frac{5i}{n})}^{2/3})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c1c8dc-2755-5935-8556-491651e97cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[4i-{i}^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c1c8dc-2755-5935-8556-491651e97cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[4i-{i}^{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261475} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e9a678-5f4e-500c-953f-a73fa6f4ca0f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e56e905-eee3-57de-a2c7-f867a2f2c170" :question/math-academy-id "q-73174" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/df177a0a9115e64a238168fea9749475de01fe6ed46d91f7f1443aca4110c6e6.png)\n\nThe area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ between $x=-2$, $x=1$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a right Riemann sum, as shown above. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=1,$ so the step size is \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(-2+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i-2) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[4-(i-2)^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[4-({i}^{2}-4i+4)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}[4i-{i}^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8642350-2991-5893-83fc-a1e23a9a6de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8642350-2991-5893-83fc-a1e23a9a6de7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261478} :answer-field/id #uuid "94bbf047-d851-5069-9d13-32de162deb7d" :answer-field/key "b" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59c11097-0528-5954-9433-d9c9f67d6be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59c11097-0528-5954-9433-d9c9f67d6be0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261480} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ed86a1f-eab0-54d3-89ce-90c324fae487" :answer-field/key "f-of-x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261479}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc6ae514-8e76-5ad4-a9b5-b333e0e65f07" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$\n\nF of x: {{f-of-x}}\n\n$b$: {{b}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbb8275b-303f-58b6-8c30-250c676a4848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbb8275b-303f-58b6-8c30-250c676a4848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261489} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b1e11d-a6bb-5b0f-98b2-5b8a88660fd4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261488}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd926104-dccb-5ceb-b205-b99c25d134c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73208" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y={e}^{x}$ between $x=1$ and $x=5$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is  \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$  with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+\\frac{4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{e}^{(1+\\frac{4j}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{e}^{1}\\cdot {e}^{4j/n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4e}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\sqrt[n]{{e}^{4j}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad7537d-f6e7-5989-8cf2-5cf56c3faf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad7537d-f6e7-5989-8cf2-5cf56c3faf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261595} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fe39afa-6dd9-5a58-868c-a6262488088c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e1ae559-045a-5306-980a-69b5a1a67c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75836" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{'}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7870ef2-531d-5e69-8fe6-b3df229d47b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7870ef2-531d-5e69-8fe6-b3df229d47b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261604} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b3f77d-3a76-59df-a100-71047bf5f189" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261603}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b51c1b7a-17ec-53ff-b412-8378d563972d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76105" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{'}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{'}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c036d9f8-2094-53ec-a5ef-9890870c32e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c036d9f8-2094-53ec-a5ef-9890870c32e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262138} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d4a264-18c3-518b-ab77-298f305d6cfb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ea85114-fc44-5f8d-8a07-b792a8223b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-99333" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}) & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89b9224-71e0-5c90-8e9e-5fc3f7e29574" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit of a Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbdd00f8-d6c8-58ae-a411-eda5b61fc994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd00f8-d6c8-58ae-a411-eda5b61fc994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262180} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ab6211-9794-517c-b032-28f71de2dc29" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "92312bfb-76e8-54fe-a82d-2fa4fc336cb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99942" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
